// Practice quiz: Testing with pytest
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/python-fundamentals/testing-with-pytest

::o1 discovery::Which file and function will python -m pytest collect as a test by default? {
~def check_total() in test_cart.py #Not quite. The function name must start with test.
~def test_total() in cart_checks.py #Not quite. cart_checks.py does not match test_*.py or *_test.py.
~def TestTotal() in cart.py #Not quite. Neither the file nor the function follows the naming rule.
=def test_total() in test_cart.py #Correct. pytest collects test_*.py (or *_test.py) files and functions whose names start with test.
}

::o1 plain assert::How do you check in a pytest test that total([2, 3]) equals 5? {
~assertEquals(5, total([2, 3])) #Not quite. That is JUnit style; it is not defined in plain pytest tests.
=assert total([2, 3]) \=\= 5 #Correct. pytest uses the plain assert statement and explains failures itself.
~self.assertEqual(total([2, 3]), 5) #Not quite. That is the unittest TestCase style; a plain pytest function has no self.
~return total([2, 3]) \=\= 5 #Not quite. A returned value is not a check; pytest warns that the test returned a value instead of asserting.
}

::o1 run one test::Which command runs only test_total in test_cart.py? {
~python -m pytest test_total #Not quite. pytest treats this as a path, and no file is named test_total.
~python test_cart.py test_total #Not quite. Running the file as a script does not run pytest at all.
=python -m pytest test_cart.py\:\:test_total #Correct. A node id names the file and the test, joined by \:\:.
~python -m pytest -q #Not quite. -q only shortens the report; it still runs every test.
}

::o2 compared values::A report shows\: E assert 9 \=\= 10, then E + where 9 \= total([4, 5]). What does it tell you? {
~The test expected 9 and got 10 #Not quite. The left side, 9, is what total([4, 5]) returned.
=total([4, 5]) returned 9, but the test expected 10 #Correct. The E lines show the actual value, the expected value and where the actual value came from.
~total crashed with an exception #Not quite. The call returned 9; only the assert failed.
~Two tests failed, one with 9 and one with 10 #Not quite. These lines belong to one failed assert.
}

::o2 failing line::In a pytest failure report, which line is the assert that failed? {
=The source line marked with a greater-than sign at the start #Correct. pytest marks the failing line of the test that way, and the E lines below it show the values.
~The first line of the report #Not quite. The first lines are headers and separators.
~Every line starting with def #Not quite. def lines show where the test function starts.
~The last line, with the totals #Not quite. The totals line counts results; it does not show the assert.
}

::o3 expect an exception::Which test passes only if parse_qty("x") raises ValueError? {
=with pytest.raises(ValueError)\: parse_qty("x") #Correct. The block fails the test unless ValueError is raised inside it.
~assert parse_qty("x") \=\= ValueError #Not quite. This compares a return value with the class ValueError; the raised error would fail the test.
~try\: parse_qty("x") except ValueError\: pass #Not quite. This also passes when nothing is raised.
~parse_qty("x") #Not quite. The ValueError would make this test fail.
}

::o3 fixture::@pytest.fixture def cart()\: return Cart(). How does a test get the cart? {
~By calling cart() inside the test #Not quite. Fixtures are requested by parameter name, not called directly.
~By importing cart from pytest #Not quite. The fixture is defined in your test code, not in pytest.
=By naming it as a parameter\: def test_add(cart)\: #Correct. pytest sees the parameter name, runs the fixture and passes in its return value.
~By adding @pytest.fixture to the test too #Not quite. The decorator marks the fixture function only.
}

::o3 parametrize::What does @pytest.mark.parametrize("n, sq", [(2, 4), (3, 9)]) do to def test_square(n, sq)? {
~Runs the test once with n and sq set to the whole list #Not quite. Each tuple becomes one run.
~Creates two fixtures named n and sq #Not quite. parametrize supplies argument values, not fixtures you define.
~Runs only the first tuple unless -q is given #Not quite. Every tuple runs; -q only shortens the report.
=Runs the test twice, once per tuple, reporting each case separately #Correct. Each tuple supplies n and sq for one run.
}
